add(
  'What is an isomorphism of vector spaces, and how do you show two spaces are isomorphic?',
  '### Definition A linear map T : V -> W is an **isomorphism** if it is bijective (injective and surjective). The inverse T^{-1} : W -> V is automatically linear. Two vector spaces V and W are **isomorphic** (V ≅ W) if there exists an isomorphism between them. ### Key theorem (finite-dimensional) Two finite-dimensional vector spaces over the same field F are isomorphic iff they have the same dimension. In particular, any n-dimensional vector space over F is isomorphic to F^n. ### Proof sketch (=>) If T : V -> W is an isomorphism, then T maps a basis of V to a basis of W (LI preserved by injectivity, spanning by surjectivity), so dim V = dim W. (<=) If dim V = dim W = n, pick bases {v_i} of V and {w_i} of W. Define T(sum c_i v_i) = sum c_i w_i. This is well-defined (coordinates are unique), linear, injective (T(v) = 0 => all c_i = 0 => v = 0), and surjective (any w = sum c_i w_i = T(sum c_i v_i)). So T is an isomorphism. ### Examples (1) **R^n ≅ M_{n×1}** (column vectors). The isomorphism sends (x_1, ..., x_n) to the column matrix [x_1; ...; x_n]. (2) **P_n ≅ R^{n+1}** via coefficients: a_0 + a_1 x + ... + a_n x^n -> (a_0, a_1, ..., a_n). (3) **M_{m,n}(R) ≅ R^{mn}** via "vectorization" (stacking columns). (4) **Sym_n ≅ R^{n(n+1)/2}** (using only the upper-triangular entries). (5) **C ≅ R^2** as real vector spaces (via a + bi -> (a, b)). (6) **C^n ≅ R^{2n}** as real vector spaces (split real and imaginary parts). ### Coordinate isomorphism Given a basis B = {b_1, ..., b_n} of V, the map v -> [v]_B (coordinates) is an isomorphism V -> F^n. This is the canonical isomorphism used to translate abstract linear algebra into matrix computations. ### Properties of isomorphisms (1) Composition of isomorphisms is an isomorphism. (2) Inverse of an isomorphism is an isomorphism. (3) Isomorphism preserves: linear independence, span, dimension, basis, subspace structure, kernel/image dimensions. (4) Isomorphisms form a group under composition (the general linear group GL(V) for V -> V automorphisms). ### Infinite-dimensional Two infinite-dimensional spaces are isomorphic iff they have the same dimension (cardinality of a basis). E.g., l^2 and L^2[0, 1] are both separable Hilbert spaces, both have countable orthonormal bases, so they\'re isomorphic as Hilbert spaces (any separable infinite-dim Hilbert space is isomorphic to l^2). ### Important note Isomorphism preserves the vector space structure but NOT additional structure (norm, inner product, topology) unless explicitly stated. E.g., R^n with the standard inner product is isomorphic as a vector space to R^n with the discrete topology, but they\'re very different as topological vector spaces.',
  'ch09p2_isomorphism',
  'formula_recall',
  ['isomorphism', 'bijective linear map', 'same dimension', 'coordinates', 'general linear group', 'Hilbert space isomorphism']
)
add(
  'Verify that the solution set of y\'\' + y = 0 is a 2-dimensional vector space and find a basis.',
  '### Setup The ODE y\'\' + y = 0 has solutions y : R -> R. We claim the solution set S = {y in C^infinity(R) : y\'\' + y = 0} is a vector subspace of C^infinity(R). ### Subspace test (1) Zero: y = 0 satisfies 0\'\' + 0 = 0, so 0 in S. (2) Closed under +: if y_1, y_2 in S, then (y_1 + y_2)\'\' + (y_1 + y_2) = y_1\'\' + y_2\'\' + y_1 + y_2 = (y_1\'\' + y_1) + (y_2\'\' + y_2) = 0 + 0 = 0, so y_1 + y_2 in S. (3) Closed under scalar mult: if y in S and c in R, then (c y)\'\' + c y = c (y\'\' + y) = c * 0 = 0, so c y in S. So S is a subspace ✓. ### Find solutions (characteristic equation) Try y = e^{rx}: y\'\' = r^2 e^{rx}, so r^2 + 1 = 0 => r = ±i (complex roots). For real solutions, use Euler\'s formula: e^{ix} = cos x + i sin x. The general complex solution is y = C_1 e^{ix} + C_2 e^{-ix}; for real solutions, take real and imaginary parts: y = A cos x + B sin x, A, B in R. ### Verify cos and sin are solutions (cos x)\'\' = -cos x, so (cos x)\'\' + cos x = 0 ✓. (sin x)\'\' = -sin x, so (sin x)\'\' + sin x = 0 ✓. ### Linear independence (Wronskian) W(cos x, sin x)(x) = det [[cos x sin x]; [-sin x cos x]] = cos^2 x + sin^2 x = 1 != 0 for all x. So {cos x, sin x} are LI on any interval. (They\'re also solutions of the linear ODE y\'\' + y = 0, so the Wronskian test applies directly.) ### Spanning For any solution y of y\'\' + y = 0, by existence/uniqueness of ODEs, y is uniquely determined by initial conditions y(0) = a, y\'(0) = b. The function y_0(x) = a cos x + b sin x satisfies y_0(0) = a, y_0\'(0) = b, and is a solution. By uniqueness, y = y_0 = a cos x + b sin x. So {cos x, sin x} spans S. ### Conclusion S is a 2-dim vector space with basis {cos x, sin x}. ### Generalization For the n-th order linear ODE y^{(n)} + p_{n-1}(x) y^{(n-1)} + ... + p_0(x) y = 0 with continuous coefficients on [a, b], the solution set is an n-dimensional vector space (a "fundamental set" of n LI solutions forms a basis). This is why ODE theory and linear algebra are deeply intertwined.',
  'ch09p2_worked_ode_solution_space',
  'problem_solving',
  ['worked', 'ODE solution space', 'subspace', 'characteristic equation', 'Wronskian', 'fundamental set', 'cos sin basis']
)
add(
  'Determine whether the vectors v_1 = (1, 2, 1), v_2 = (2, 1, 0), v_3 = (4, 5, 2) are linearly independent, and find a basis for their span.',
  '### Setup Three vectors in R^3. Form the matrix A with these as columns: A = [[1 2 4]; [2 1 5]; [1 0 2]]. ### Test 1: determinant det(A) = 1*(1*2 - 5*0) - 2*(2*2 - 5*1) + 4*(2*0 - 1*1) = 1*2 - 2*(4 - 5) + 4*(0 - 1) = 2 - 2*(-1) + 4*(-1) = 2 + 2 - 4 = 0. det = 0 => vectors are linearly DEPENDENT (and do not span R^3). ### Test 2: RREF [[1 2 4]; [2 1 5]; [1 0 2]]. R2 -> R2 - 2 R1: [[1 2 4]; [0 -3 -3]; [1 0 2]]. R3 -> R3 - R1: [[1 2 4]; [0 -3 -3]; [0 -2 -2]]. R3 -> R3 - (2/3) R2: [[1 2 4]; [0 -3 -3]; [0 0 0]]. R2 -> R2 / (-3): [[1 2 4]; [0 1 1]; [0 0 0]]. R1 -> R1 - 2 R2: [[1 0 2]; [0 1 1]; [0 0 0]]. RREF done. Two pivots (cols 1, 2), so rank = 2. ### Linear dependence The third column of the RREF is (2, 1, 0)^T, which means v_3 = 2 v_1 + 1 v_2 + 0 v_3 (no contribution from itself in RREF terms — wait, this is the dependence relation in the original column space). Actually, the third column of RREF being (2, 1, 0)^T means: v_3 = 2 * (first pivot column of A) + 1 * (second pivot column of A) + 0 * (third column). So v_3 = 2 v_1 + 1 v_2 = 2 (1, 2, 1) + 1 (2, 1, 0) = (2, 4, 2) + (2, 1, 0) = (4, 5, 2) ✓. So the dependence relation is 2 v_1 + v_2 - v_3 = 0 (i.e., v_3 = 2 v_1 + v_2). ### Basis for span(v_1, v_2, v_3) Since v_3 is a linear combination of v_1, v_2, the span is 2-dimensional with basis {v_1, v_2} (the pivot columns of A). Any 2 of the 3 vectors that are LI would also form a basis; here {v_1, v_2} works (and they\'re LI since neither is a multiple of the other). ### Geometric interpretation The span is a 2-dim subspace of R^3 — a plane through the origin. The plane contains the points (1, 2, 1), (2, 1, 0), and (4, 5, 2), as well as their linear combinations. To find the equation of the plane: find a normal vector n perpendicular to v_1 and v_2: n = v_1 × v_2 = (2*0 - 1*1, 1*2 - 1*0, 1*1 - 2*2) = (-1, 2, -3). Plane: -x + 2y - 3z = 0. Check: -1 + 4 - 3 = 0 ✓ (for v_1); -2 + 2 - 0 = 0 ✓ (for v_2); -4 + 10 - 6 = 0 ✓ (for v_3). So span = {(x, y, z) : -x + 2y - 3z = 0}, a 2-dim plane in R^3.',
  'ch09p2_worked_li_basis_span',
  'problem_solving',
  ['worked', 'linear independence', 'RREF', 'pivot columns', 'dependence relation', 'basis for span', 'plane']
)
add(
  'Find the matrix of the linear transformation T(p) = p\' (derivative) on P_2 relative to the standard basis, and find its kernel and image.',
  '### Setup T : P_2 -> P_2 (actually T : P_2 -> P_1, but we can view it as P_2 -> P_2 with the image landing in P_1 ⊆ P_2) by T(p) = p\' (derivative). Standard basis of P_2: B = {1, x, x^2}. ### Compute T on each basis vector T(1) = 0 = 0 * 1 + 0 * x + 0 * x^2 -> coordinates (0, 0, 0)^T. T(x) = 1 = 1 * 1 + 0 * x + 0 * x^2 -> (1, 0, 0)^T. T(x^2) = 2x = 0 * 1 + 2 * x + 0 * x^2 -> (0, 2, 0)^T. ### Matrix representation [T]_B = [[0 1 0]; [0 0 2]; [0 0 0]] (columns are [T(1)]_B, [T(x)]_B, [T(x^2)]_B). This is a 3x3 matrix (square) but only the first two rows are nonzero — the image lands in P_1 (subspace spanned by 1, x). ### Properties (1) Upper triangular (strictly, in fact). (2) Nilpotent: [T]^2 = [[0 0 2]; [0 0 0]; [0 0 0]], [T]^3 = 0 (differentiation applied 3 times kills any quadratic polynomial). (3) Eigenvalues: all 0 (since nilpotent). (4) Characteristic polynomial: lambda^3 (only eigenvalue 0 with multiplicity 3). (5) NOT diagonalizable (the only eigenvalue is 0 but the matrix is not the zero matrix — would need to be diagonal 0). ### Kernel ker(T) = {p : p\' = 0} = {constant polynomials} = span{1}. Dimension 1. From the matrix: ker([T]) = null space = {c : [T] c = 0} = {c : (c_2, 2 c_3, 0) = (0, 0, 0)} = {c : c_2 = 0, c_3 = 0, c_1 free}. So ker = span{(1, 0, 0)^T} = span{1} ✓. ### Image im(T) = {p\' : p in P_2} = {a + 2b x : a, b in R} = P_1 (polynomials of degree <= 1). Dimension 2 = rank(T). From the matrix: column space = span{(0,0,0)^T, (1,0,0)^T, (0,2,0)^T} = span{(1,0,0)^T, (0,1,0)^T} = span{1, x} = P_1 ✓. ### Rank-nullity check dim P_2 = 3. nullity = 1 (ker = span{1}). rank = 2 (im = P_1, dim 2). 1 + 2 = 3 ✓. ### Generalization For T : P_n -> P_n given by differentiation: [T]_B is the (n+1) x (n+1) matrix with 1, 2, 3, ..., n on the superdiagonal (and 0 elsewhere). It\'s nilpotent of index n+1. The kernel is span{1} (constant polynomials), the image is P_{n-1}, rank = n, nullity = 1, and dim P_n = n + 1 = 1 + n ✓.',
  'ch09p2_worked_derivative_matrix',
  'problem_solving',
  ['worked', 'derivative operator', 'matrix representation', 'nilpotent', 'kernel', 'image', 'P_n', 'rank-nullity']
)
add(
  'Apply Gram-Schmidt to the columns of A = [[1 1 1]; [1 1 0]; [1 0 0]] to obtain a QR decomposition.',
  '### Setup A is 3x3 with columns v_1 = (1, 1, 1), v_2 = (1, 1, 0), v_3 = (1, 0, 0). Apply Gram-Schmidt to obtain orthonormal q_1, q_2, q_3 and upper-triangular R with A = Q R. ### Step 1: q_1 u_1 = v_1 = (1, 1, 1). ||u_1|| = sqrt(3). q_1 = (1/sqrt(3)) (1, 1, 1). r_11 = ||u_1|| = sqrt(3) (or = <v_1, q_1> = (1+1+1)/sqrt(3) = sqrt(3) ✓). ### Step 2: q_2 <v_2, q_1> = (1+1+0)/sqrt(3) = 2/sqrt(3). u_2 = v_2 - <v_2, q_1> q_1 = (1, 1, 0) - (2/sqrt(3))(1/sqrt(3))(1, 1, 1) = (1, 1, 0) - (2/3)(1, 1, 1) = (1/3, 1/3, -2/3). ||u_2||^2 = 1/9 + 1/9 + 4/9 = 6/9 = 2/3, ||u_2|| = sqrt(2/3) = sqrt(2)/sqrt(3). q_2 = u_2 / ||u_2|| = (1/3, 1/3, -2/3) / (sqrt(2)/sqrt(3)) = (sqrt(3)/(3 sqrt(2))) (1, 1, -2) = (1/sqrt(6)) (1, 1, -2). r_12 = <v_2, q_1> = 2/sqrt(3). r_22 = ||u_2|| = sqrt(2/3). ### Step 3: q_3 <v_3, q_1> = (1+0+0)/sqrt(3) = 1/sqrt(3). <v_3, q_2> = (1/sqrt(6))(1+0+0) = 1/sqrt(6). u_3 = v_3 - <v_3, q_1> q_1 - <v_3, q_2> q_2 = (1, 0, 0) - (1/sqrt(3))(1/sqrt(3))(1, 1, 1) - (1/sqrt(6))(1/sqrt(6))(1, 1, -2) = (1, 0, 0) - (1/3)(1, 1, 1) - (1/6)(1, 1, -2) = (1, 0, 0) - (1/3, 1/3, 1/3) - (1/6, 1/6, -1/3) = (1 - 1/3 - 1/6, 0 - 1/3 - 1/6, 0 - 1/3 + 1/3) = (1/2, -1/2, 0). ||u_3||^2 = 1/4 + 1/4 + 0 = 1/2, ||u_3|| = 1/sqrt(2). q_3 = u_3 / (1/sqrt(2)) = sqrt(2) (1/2, -1/2, 0) = (1/sqrt(2)) (1, -1, 0). r_13 = <v_3, q_1> = 1/sqrt(3). r_23 = <v_3, q_2> = 1/sqrt(6). r_33 = ||u_3|| = 1/sqrt(2). ### Result Q = [[1/sqrt(3) 1/sqrt(6) 1/sqrt(2)]; [1/sqrt(3) 1/sqrt(6) -1/sqrt(2)]; [1/sqrt(3) -2/sqrt(6) 0]]. R = [[sqrt(3) 2/sqrt(3) 1/sqrt(3)]; [0 sqrt(2/3) 1/sqrt(6)]; [0 0 1/sqrt(2)]]. ### Verify Q is orthogonal Q^T Q should be I. <q_1, q_1> = 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3 = 1 ✓. <q_1, q_2> = (1 + 1 - 2)/(sqrt(3) sqrt(6)) = 0 ✓. <q_1, q_3> = (1 - 1 + 0)/(sqrt(3) sqrt(2)) = 0 ✓. <q_2, q_2> = (1 + 1 + 4)/6 = 1 ✓. <q_2, q_3> = (1 - 1 + 0)/(sqrt(6) sqrt(2)) = 0 ✓. <q_3, q_3> = (1 + 1 + 0)/2 = 1 ✓. ### Verify A = Q R First column of Q R: Q [sqrt(3); 0; 0] = sqrt(3) q_1 = (1, 1, 1) = v_1 ✓. Second column: Q [2/sqrt(3); sqrt(2/3); 0] = (2/sqrt(3)) q_1 + sqrt(2/3) q_2 = (2/sqrt(3))(1/sqrt(3))(1,1,1) + sqrt(2/3)(1/sqrt(6))(1,1,-2) = (2/3)(1,1,1) + (1/3)(1,1,-2) = (1, 1, 0) = v_2 ✓. Third column: Q [1/sqrt(3); 1/sqrt(6); 1/sqrt(2)] = (1/sqrt(3)) q_1 + (1/sqrt(6)) q_2 + (1/sqrt(2)) q_3 = (1/3)(1,1,1) + (1/6)(1,1,-2) + (1/2)(1,-1,0) = (1/3 + 1/6 + 1/2, 1/3 + 1/6 - 1/2, 1/3 - 1/3 + 0) = (1, 0, 0) = v_3 ✓.',
  'ch09p2_worked_qr_decomposition',
  'problem_solving',
  ['worked', 'QR decomposition', 'Gram-Schmidt', 'orthonormal columns', 'upper triangular', 'verification']
)

